    "description": "The warehouse system accepts reservation commands for InventoryItem records. Each InventoryItem contains an on-hand quantity and a collection of Allocation records, where each Allocation names a Shipment by its ShipmentId and records a reserved quantity. The business rule is that the sum of active allocations for an item must never exceed its on-hand quantity. Two operators may reserve stock concurrently, and a failed command must not leave only some allocation changes persisted. Shipment records are maintained by another part of the system and are referenced by identity rather than modified during a reservation. The storage layer currently supports saving individual Allocation rows, which allows concurrent callers to interleave updates and violate the quantity rule. A command must load the relevant inventory record, apply the requested reservation to the records it owns, reject insufficient stock, and persist the complete change as one unit. The design must identify the narrowest boundary in which the quantity invariant can always be enforced.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the ownership and transaction boundary that ensures active reservations for each inventory item can never exceed the available quantity under concurrent commands.",
    "notes": "aggregate primary: the inventory item plus its allocations form the ownership and transaction boundary enforcing the quantity invariant under concurrency; domain-service is the stateless-operation near-miss; entity and application-service support."
  },
